# /etc/kitchen-sink/journal-ignore.regex: known-harmless journal errors.
#
# kitchen-checkup reads the journal lines of priority err and worse since its
# last run, as "identifier: message", and drops each line that matches one of
# the extended regexes below, one per line. Lines starting with # and blank
# lines are skipped. Keep patterns narrow: a broad one hides real problems.
# Sudo authentication failures are deliberately not listed, except the
# updater's own tagged probes at the end.

# systemd 262 cannot set up NvPCRs on the AMD fTPM (the three known failed units)
NvPCR
Failed to start (TPM NvPCR|Early TPM SRK Setup)

# Firmware and kernel noise at every boot
ACPI (BIOS )?Error
virt/tdx: TDX not supported
^kernel: *$

# Login and session noise
gkr-pam: couldn.t unlock the login keyring
Activation request for .org\.freedesktop\.nm_dispatcher. failed
Invalid user name .passim. in service file
run_pending_traps: bad value in trap_list
uwsm: PID [0-9]+ exited with RC 143
mm_reap: child terminated by signal 15

# At every shutdown; coredumpctl shows no real crash behind it
Failed to send coredump datagram

# The check-up and the updater report their own results
^kitchen-checkup:
^kitchen-update:

# The updater's own sudo probes: its grant self-test and its revoke proof each
# expect one refused `sudo -n` a night, which sudo logs at alert priority. They
# carry a tag no other command uses; any other refused sudo still shows.
^sudo: .* : a password is required ; .*COMMAND=/usr/bin/true kitchen-update-probe$
